const jobPrepCases = {
    Resume: [
        ["Easy","Turn a task into a resume bullet","You built a profile form with validation. Rewrite the vague bullet 'Worked on profile page' as a truthful action-and-result bullet without inventing metrics.","A strong version names your contribution and evidence: 'Built a profile form with client-side required-field feedback and connected it to the existing update API.' Add a measured result only if you have one.","What if the work was shared with a teammate?","State your part precisely and credit collaboration; do not imply sole ownership."],
        ["Easy","Choose a readable resume structure","A new graduate has coursework, two projects, and a short internship. Recommend a one-page section order for a frontend role and explain what should receive the most space.","Use concise contact/header, skills supported by evidence, relevant experience/projects, then education. Put the strongest role-relevant project or experience early; omit filler objective text and keep dates and formatting consistent.","What changes after several years of relevant experience?","Move relevant professional experience above projects and reduce coursework detail."],
        ["Easy","Check a resume claim for evidence","A draft says 'Expert in cloud security' after one tutorial. Identify the credibility problem and rewrite the claim honestly using observable evidence.","The claim exceeds the evidence. Name the actual experience, such as 'Completed a guided deployment exercise using IAM roles and documented the access policy,' and avoid an expertise label unsupported by repeated practice.","How can a reviewer verify the claim?","Link a relevant project or describe a concrete artifact and the specific work performed."],
        ["Medium","Tailor a resume to a job description","A role emphasizes React, accessible forms, and API integration. You have one project demonstrating all three and several unrelated tools in the skills list. Explain how you would tailor the resume without keyword stuffing.","Prioritize the relevant project, describe the accessible form and API work in accurate bullets, and list only skills you can discuss. Preserve the underlying facts; reorder emphasis rather than copying the posting's phrases without evidence.","Should you claim every listed framework because it is a keyword?","No. Include only tools used enough to explain your contribution and limitations."],
        ["Medium","Handle a career gap accurately","A candidate has a six-month gap during which they studied and cared for family. They ask whether to hide the dates. Recommend a concise, privacy-respecting approach for the resume and interview.","Use accurate dates; do not fabricate employment. The resume may omit a personal explanation unless useful, while an interview answer can briefly state the period and relevant learning without disclosing private family details.","What if the gap involved a confidential health issue?","Share only what the candidate chooses; redirect to readiness and relevant qualifications."],
        ["Medium","Choose metrics responsibly","A project has no analytics, but the candidate wants to quantify impact. Explain which metrics can be reported and how to present a local test result without implying production scale.","Use only reproducible measurements with scope and method, e.g. 'Reduced a local test fixture from 900 ms to 240 ms across 20 runs.' If no reliable measure exists, describe delivered behavior instead of inventing user or revenue impact.","What baseline is needed for a performance claim?","Comparable inputs, environment, method, and before/after observations."],
        ["Hard","Resolve a resume versus portfolio discrepancy","A resume says a project used MongoDB transactions, but the public repository shows a single-document update. Explain how to investigate and fix the mismatch before applying.","Verify the actual implementation and commit history, correct the resume if the claim is unsupported, or update the project only if the feature is genuinely required and tested. Ensure the portfolio description, code, and interview explanation agree.","Should the candidate keep the impressive claim while planning to add it later?","No. Future intent is not completed experience; describe it as planned only when relevant."],
        ["Hard","Build an evidence-based skills section","A candidate lists 30 technologies but can demonstrate depth in six. Propose a way to group and rank skills for a backend role while avoiding proficiency ratings that have no defined basis.","Group role-relevant languages, backend frameworks, data stores, and tooling; place demonstrated skills first and remove incidental exposure. Tie each important skill to a project or work example and avoid vague star ratings.","How should a skill learned recently be represented?","Include it only if relevant and be ready to state the scope and limits of practice."],
        ["Hard","Review a resume for privacy and trust","A draft includes full home address, birth date, government ID, references' phone numbers, and a public link to a private document. Identify the risks and a safer review checklist.","Remove unnecessary sensitive identifiers and third-party contact details, use a professional contact method, verify the share link's audience and permissions, and check file metadata. Share only information necessary for hiring and authorized references.","What if an application explicitly requests a sensitive field?","Verify the request and legitimate channel, provide only what is required, and avoid embedding it in a broadly shared resume."],
    ],
    "GitHub Profile": [
        ["Easy","Improve a repository's first impression","A recruiter opens a project repository and sees no description, no README, and an unclear default branch. List the three highest-value first changes and explain what each helps a reviewer understand.","Add a concise repository description, a README with purpose/setup/demo, and a clear default branch with a clean entry point. These reduce discovery friction without claiming the project is production-ready.","What should the README do if there is no live demo?","Show screenshots or sample output and state that the project runs locally."],
        ["Easy","Select projects to pin","A profile can pin six repositories: a polished API, an unfinished tutorial fork, a UI project, a small algorithm-practice repo, and two archived experiments. Explain which you would feature for a full-stack role and why.","Feature the API and UI project plus the strongest complete supporting work that demonstrates breadth. Avoid pinning an unfinished tutorial fork as original work; archive or clearly label experiments instead of presenting them as finished products.","Should all six slots be filled?","No. Empty space is better than weak or misleading projects."],
        ["Easy","Write a useful repository description","Rewrite 'My project' as a concise description for a task planner that supports authenticated CRUD and filtering, without implying a user base.","For example: 'Task planner with authenticated CRUD, category filters, and a React client backed by an Express API.' It states capabilities, not adoption or business success.","Which claim would require separate evidence?","Any number of active users, uptime, or performance improvement."],
        ["Medium","Audit a repository before sharing","You plan to make a class project public. Explain how to check for credentials, private data, generated files, and third-party licenses before changing visibility.","Search history as well as current files for secrets, remove personal data, rotate any exposed credential, review ignored/build artifacts and licenses, and confirm collaborators authorize publication. Deleting a secret from the latest commit alone does not erase history.","What if a token was committed months ago and then deleted?","Treat it as compromised, revoke/rotate it, and assess history cleanup and provider audit logs."],
        ["Medium","Make project activity honest","A candidate wants a green contribution graph and considers splitting one change into many empty commits. Recommend a professional alternative that shows real maintenance.","Make meaningful commits tied to actual work: tests, documentation, fixes, or issues. A clear changelog and complete project are stronger signals than artificial commit volume; do not manufacture activity.","What if the project is stable and needs no change?","Document its maintenance status and focus on a new useful contribution elsewhere."],
        ["Medium","Document how to reproduce a bug","A repository issue says 'login broken' with no steps. Draft the minimum reproducible report fields that help another developer investigate without exposing a real user's credentials.","Include expected versus actual behavior, safe reproduction steps, environment/version, relevant sanitized logs, and whether it is reproducible. Use a test account and redact tokens, email addresses, and private payloads.","Where should a sensitive security report go?","Use the project's private security contact/process rather than a public issue."],
        ["Hard","Separate original work from a fork","A portfolio repo began as a tutorial starter and now includes significant changes. Explain how to disclose provenance, identify your contributions, and avoid implying you authored upstream code.","Keep the upstream attribution/license, state the starter source, summarize your original changes, and link to commits or a contribution section. Do not remove license notices or pass inherited code off as original.","What if the upstream license is unclear?","Do not publish until the license and reuse rights are understood."],
        ["Hard","Design a maintainable project README","A reviewer should be able to understand, run, test, and assess a repository. Propose README sections and explain how setup instructions remain trustworthy as dependencies evolve.","Include purpose/scope, screenshots or API examples, prerequisites, environment setup without secrets, install/run/test steps, architecture notes, limitations, and license. Validate commands in CI or a clean checkout and update docs with behavior changes.","How do you document environment variables safely?","List names, purpose, and sample placeholders; never include real values."],
        ["Hard","Investigate an unexpectedly large repository","A clone is 800 MB although the app is small. Describe a safe investigation and cleanup plan that does not accidentally remove source assets or rewrite shared history without coordination.","Inspect largest tracked files and Git history, generated artifacts, and dependency bundles; confirm which assets are reproducible. Remove only confirmed artifacts, add appropriate ignore rules, and coordinate history rewriting because it affects collaborators and existing clones.","Does adding a file to .gitignore remove it from history?","No. It only affects untracked files; tracked history requires a separate, coordinated cleanup."],
    ],
    Portfolio: [
        ["Easy","Choose a portfolio homepage hierarchy","A portfolio homepage shows a giant animation before the candidate's name or work. Recommend a first-screen content order that helps a recruiter quickly judge relevance.","Show name and role focus, a concise value statement, one primary project or work sample, and clear contact/resume links. Keep animation secondary and honor reduced-motion preferences.","What if the candidate has no professional work yet?","Feature complete personal or coursework projects and label their context honestly."],
        ["Easy","Write a project case-study summary","A project card says only 'Built with React and Node.' Write the information a visitor needs before deciding to open the case study.","Summarize the user problem, what the candidate personally built, the main technical challenge, and a link to a working demo or repository when available. Avoid unsupported claims such as 'industry-leading.'","Which details belong in the full case study?","Architecture, tradeoffs, screenshots, validation, and lessons can follow in the detail view."],
        ["Easy","Check a portfolio link","A project button opens a blank page because its URL is empty. Explain how to represent unavailable demos honestly while keeping repository access clear.","Do not render a dead or placeholder link. Show a disabled or omitted demo action with a clear note and provide the repository or local setup instructions if available.","Should a disabled link still have href='#'?","No. It creates confusing navigation; render text or a properly disabled control."],
        ["Medium","Audit accessibility in a portfolio project","A project demo uses color alone for errors, keyboard-inaccessible menus, and unlabeled icon buttons. Prioritize fixes and name a manual test sequence.","Associate textual errors with fields, ensure menus and buttons work with keyboard and visible focus, add accessible names, and check contrast. Test tab order, activation with Enter/Space, and screen-reader labels rather than relying only on color.","What should happen to focus after a dialog closes?","Return focus to the control that opened it."],
        ["Medium","Improve portfolio performance responsibly","A page loads a 12 MB hero video and several full-size screenshots. Propose improvements while preserving useful visual evidence and avoiding invented performance claims.","Use an optimized responsive poster/image, compress and size assets, lazy-load below-the-fold media, and avoid autoplaying heavy video. Measure with a repeatable lab/network profile and report the environment and metric.","What if the animation explains an important interaction?","Provide a lightweight preview plus a user-initiated detailed demo and accessible equivalent."],
        ["Medium","Handle a project with no public backend","A portfolio demo cannot expose its private API credentials. Explain safe presentation options that let reviewers inspect behavior without putting secrets in the frontend.","Use a controlled demo backend with restricted test data and server-held credentials, a local run guide with placeholders, or a recorded walkthrough. Never embed a secret in a client bundle or use real user data.","Can a public demo use a low-privilege test key in React?","No secret in client code is private; proxy through a server and enforce limits."],
        ["Hard","Present a project with a known limitation","A project has no mobile layout and the candidate has not fixed it. Explain how to disclose the limitation constructively and define a credible next step.","State the limitation, its impact, and the tradeoff or time constraint honestly. Describe a concrete next step such as responsive breakpoints and a device test plan; do not imply the feature is already complete.","Would it be better to hide the project?","Consider whether it demonstrates relevant strengths, but label its state and avoid presenting unfinished behavior as complete."],
        ["Hard","Design a trustworthy portfolio case study","A reviewer wants evidence of the candidate's role, decisions, and outcomes. Outline a case study that distinguishes measured results from estimates and protects employer confidentiality.","Use context, constraints, personal contribution, alternatives/tradeoffs, implementation evidence, verification, outcomes with method/source, and lessons. Anonymize confidential details and obtain permission before sharing screenshots or data.","What if the employer owns the work?","Share only authorized material and describe skills at a high level without copying proprietary code."],
        ["Hard","Review a portfolio for inclusive usability","A portfolio has low-contrast muted text, motion-heavy transitions, and narrow fixed-width layouts. Create a verification checklist for accessibility and responsive behavior.","Check contrast, semantic headings/landmarks, keyboard/focus, reduced motion, text zoom, touch targets, alt text, and layouts at representative widths. Fix content clipping and test with both keyboard and assistive technology; a screenshot alone cannot verify interaction.","Which single mobile check catches many layout defects?","Test at narrow viewport with text zoom and inspect horizontal overflow plus keyboard/focus paths."],
    ],
    "LinkedIn Profile": [
        ["Easy","Write a specific professional headline","A profile headline says 'Student | Looking for opportunities.' Rewrite its structure for an entry-level full-stack candidate without claiming seniority or certifications.","Use role direction plus verified strengths, e.g. 'Entry-level Full-Stack Developer | React, Express, MongoDB projects.' Include only skills supported by work you can explain.","Should the headline list every technology studied once?","No. Prioritize relevant, practiced skills."],
        ["Easy","Improve an About section opening","The first line of an About section says 'I am passionate and hardworking.' Replace it with a concrete role focus and evidence prompt.","Start with the kind of problems or product work you pursue, then cite one project and what you personally contributed. Keep the voice natural and avoid unverifiable superlatives.","What should the closing line invite?","A relevant conversation or collaboration, with a professional contact path."],
        ["Easy","Choose profile contact details","A candidate considers placing a personal phone number, home address, and resume link in a public profile. Recommend a privacy-conscious contact setup.","Use a professional email or approved contact method and a resume link with intentionally chosen access. Avoid publishing a home address or unnecessary personal number; review visibility settings from a signed-out view.","What could a public resume link expose?","Metadata, personal details, and access to the file beyond the intended audience."],
        ["Medium","Send a focused connection request","You met an engineer at a local event and want to ask about their team's work. Draft a brief connection request that is specific, respectful, and does not immediately demand a referral.","Mention where you met, one topic from the conversation, and a modest reason to stay connected. Keep it short and offer context rather than asking for a job in the first message.","When is it appropriate to ask for a referral?","After a genuine conversation and when the person has enough context to make an informed choice."],
        ["Medium","Make experience entries consistent","A profile lists one role with a title, another with only a company name, and a project as employment. Explain how to distinguish employment, education, and projects accurately.","Use the appropriate experience, education, or project sections; provide consistent dates and concise contributions; label freelance, volunteer, and academic work honestly. Do not imply a personal project was an employer.","What if dates are approximate?","Use truthful month/year precision or the platform's supported format; do not fabricate exact dates."],
        ["Medium","Request a recommendation ethically","A former teammate agrees to write a recommendation. Explain how to ask without scripting a false endorsement and how to protect their time.","Ask whether they are comfortable, remind them of shared work and a specific project, and let them use their own words. Provide context, not claims they cannot verify, and accept a decline graciously.","Should you offer to write it for them to paste?","No. That risks misrepresenting their independent endorsement."],
        ["Hard","Resolve conflicting public profile claims","A resume says 'backend developer' while the profile headline says 'AI researcher' and featured work shows only frontend tutorials. Propose a coherent positioning update without erasing legitimate interests.","Choose a primary current target role, align headline, About, skills, and featured projects to evidence for that role, and mention a secondary interest transparently. Update the resume and portfolio consistently.","What if the candidate is actively changing fields?","State the transition and show concrete learning/project evidence rather than presenting the new role as established experience."],
        ["Hard","Plan a privacy review of a public profile","A recruiter can view the profile, but it also exposes an old employer's client name and a family phone number. Explain a safe cleanup process and what should be verified after edits.","Review each section for third-party/confidential data, remove unauthorized details, update contact visibility, and check public view from a signed-out or alternate account. Keep only information the candidate is authorized to publish.","Can deleting the visible text remove cached copies?","Not necessarily; request removal where possible and treat prior exposure according to relevant policy."],
        ["Hard","Build a sustainable professional presence","A candidate wants to post daily but has little time and fears shallow content. Create a realistic four-week plan focused on evidence of learning and community value.","Choose a sustainable cadence such as one useful post or project update weekly, share a concrete lesson with context, engage thoughtfully with others, and review whether the activity supports career goals. Do not inflate engagement or copy others' work.","What if a week has no meaningful update?","Skip it or share a thoughtful resource with your own analysis; consistency does not require filler."],
    ],
    "Project Documentation": [
        ["Easy","Write reproducible setup steps","A README says 'install dependencies and run the app' but omits commands and environment setup. Draft the minimum details a new contributor needs, without publishing credentials.","Name runtime prerequisites, exact install and start commands, required environment variable names with placeholder examples, and how to run tests. State expected local URL/output and common setup limitations.","How should secrets be represented in examples?","Use clearly fake placeholders in an ignored local env file and explain how to obtain values securely."],
        ["Easy","Document an API endpoint","A project has POST /api/projects but no description. List the essential request, authentication, validation, success, and error information for useful endpoint documentation.","Document purpose, auth requirement, fields/types, validation constraints, success status/envelope, common 400/401/403/409 outcomes, and a redacted example. Keep docs aligned with implementation/tests.","Should an example include a real token?","No; use a placeholder and never commit credentials."],
        ["Easy","Add a clear screenshot caption","A README includes a dashboard screenshot but does not explain what it demonstrates. Write a caption that helps a reviewer while remaining accurate.","Describe the visible workflow and context, such as 'Dashboard showing saved roadmap progress for a sample account.' Use synthetic data and add alt text that conveys the relevant content.","What if the screenshot contains a real email?","Replace it with synthetic content or redact before publication."],
        ["Medium","Document an architecture decision","A team chose local browser storage for saved listings instead of adding an API. Explain what an architecture decision record should capture, including consequences and a review trigger.","Record context, decision, alternatives, consequences (device-local and not account-synced), security constraints, and a trigger for reconsideration such as adding authenticated server persistence. Keep the record concise and dated.","What wording avoids promising persistence across devices?","Explicitly say it persists only in that browser/profile and can be cleared."],
        ["Medium","Keep README instructions in sync","A package script changes but setup docs do not. Propose a low-cost process to prevent documentation drift and a test that verifies the highest-risk commands.","Update docs in the same change as scripts, use copyable commands, and add a CI smoke check that installs/builds or checks documented command names. Assign ownership for externally configured services.","Should CI call real paid providers?","Use mocks or test credentials in a controlled integration job; do not make every build depend on a paid live API."],
        ["Medium","Write a useful troubleshooting section","A new developer gets MongoDB connection failures when the service is stopped. Draft troubleshooting guidance that distinguishes common local setup issues without exposing secrets.","Check service reachability, selected database URI variable, network/firewall, and sanitized server logs; confirm env loading and restart after config changes. Never paste the full URI or credentials into a public issue.","What safe diagnostic can a user share?","A redacted error category, runtime version, and whether the database host is reachable."],
        ["Hard","Document a safe data migration","A schema migration changes a field from optional string to a validated enum. Specify preconditions, rollout, backfill handling, verification, and rollback strategy.","Inventory existing values, add tolerant read/validation support, migrate in bounded batches with counts and quarantine for unknown values, verify parity, then enforce the new constraint after compatibility. Keep a rollback path that preserves original data until the cutover is confirmed.","Why not map every unknown value to a default?","That silently changes meaning and can corrupt user data; preserve and review unknown records."],
        ["Hard","Write an incident runbook","An API outage occurs after a deployment. Outline a runbook that enables safe triage without relying on undocumented personal knowledge.","Include impact confirmation, rollback criteria, dependency checks, safe dashboards/log fields, communication owner, mitigation steps, data-integrity checks, recovery validation, and escalation contacts. Avoid credentials and record a post-incident review step.","How should runbook steps be tested?","Run a non-destructive tabletop or staging exercise and record gaps and owners."],
        ["Hard","Create privacy-conscious technical docs","A developer wants to add request/response examples from production logs to API docs. Explain how to create representative examples without exposing personal information or teaching unsafe use.","Use synthetic data, remove tokens and identifiers, document redaction, and validate examples against schemas. Include ownership/auth constraints and safe error handling; do not copy raw production payloads into documentation.","How do you ensure examples stay realistic?","Generate them from fixtures or contract tests that contain deliberately synthetic records."],
    ],
    "Job Search": [
        ["Easy","Build a focused role search","A candidate applies to every software job they find, including senior roles unrelated to their skills. Create a simple search filter based on target level, role, location/work mode, and must-have requirements.","Choose a small role family and realistic level, set location or remote constraints, and separate required qualifications from preferences. Review each listing for actual responsibilities before applying.","Should a missing optional skill automatically disqualify a role?","No. Distinguish core requirements from preferred qualifications and assess evidence honestly."],
        ["Easy","Check whether a listing is current","A listing has no posting date and its apply link redirects to an unrelated page. Describe what to verify before spending time on an application.","Check the employer's official careers site, role title/location, current application destination, and publication/closing status. If the listing cannot be verified, do not submit personal documents through the suspicious link.","What if the company website is unavailable?","Wait or use a verified official contact path; do not send sensitive data to an unverified intermediary."],
        ["Easy","Track a weekly job-search goal","A learner has two hours weekly for job search. Propose a realistic repeatable routine that favors quality and leaves a record of next steps.","Use one block to find and verify relevant roles, another to tailor a small number of applications, and reserve time for follow-up/networking. Track date, role, source, status, and next action without storing unnecessary sensitive data.","What should the goal avoid?","A large application count that rewards low-quality, untailored submissions."],
        ["Medium","Evaluate a role with mixed requirements","A junior role asks for three years of experience, but most tasks match the candidate's project work and several requirements are preferred. Explain how to decide whether to apply.","Compare essential responsibilities with demonstrated ability, identify truthful evidence and learning gaps, and consider the level/context. Apply if the core work is plausible; do not misstate years or credentials. Avoid treating every bullet as an absolute.","When should a required credential stop the application?","When it is a genuine legal or operational prerequisite, not merely a preferred tool."],
        ["Medium","Recognize a suspicious job offer","A recruiter asks for a fee, bank details, and an unverified messaging-app interview before sharing a job description. Explain the warning signs and safe verification steps.","Do not pay or share financial identity details. Verify the recruiter and role through the employer's official domain, contact the company independently, and report/block if fraud is likely. Legitimate hiring should explain the role and process.","Could a real employer ever request identity documents?","At an appropriate verified stage through a secure official process, not as an unexplained upfront fee or chat demand."],
        ["Medium","Compare two opportunities fairly","One role offers a higher salary but a long commute and unclear responsibilities; another offers lower pay, mentorship, and clear scope. Build a decision matrix without pretending one factor determines the answer.","Compare verified compensation, role fit, growth, manager/team, work arrangement, commute, stability, and personal constraints. Weight priorities explicitly, identify unknowns to ask about, and do not infer culture from marketing alone.","Which unknown should be resolved before accepting?","Any high-impact item such as actual duties, manager expectations, total compensation, or work location."],
        ["Hard","Design a search strategy for a career transition","A civil engineer is moving toward software roles and has limited time. Plan a six-week search that combines skill evidence, role selection, networking, and feedback without claiming an immediate guaranteed outcome.","Select adjacent roles and realistic entry points, build one demonstrable software project, revise materials around transferable skills, seek informational feedback, and review application response patterns weekly. Adjust based on evidence, not a promised application count.","How do you avoid abandoning the target too early?","Set review windows and assess a meaningful sample of outcomes plus quality of materials before changing direction."],
        ["Hard","Investigate a low response rate","After 30 tailored applications, a candidate receives no interviews. Propose a diagnostic plan that tests the funnel before recommending more volume.","Review role fit and eligibility, resume evidence/ATS readability, portfolio relevance, referrals, and listing freshness. Ask a qualified reviewer for blinded feedback, compare response rates by role/source, and change one variable at a time; acknowledge small-sample uncertainty.","Why not conclude the candidate is unqualified?","No response has many possible causes and is not a reliable measure of overall ability."],
        ["Hard","Protect personal data during job search","A third-party job board requests a passport scan before an interview and stores resumes publicly by default. Explain a privacy-first decision process.","Question necessity and timing, verify employer legitimacy independently, inspect visibility/retention settings, share only minimum required data through a secure channel, and decline or report suspicious requests. Review public resume links after upload.","What if the platform requires public visibility to apply?","Assess the tradeoff, use a minimal sanitized resume if acceptable, or choose a verified alternate application path."],
    ],
    "Job Applications": [
        ["Easy","Tailor one application accurately","A job asks for React forms and REST APIs. Your project used both, but the resume bullet says only 'built a web app.' Rewrite it using concrete evidence without copying the posting verbatim.","Name the feature and your contribution, such as 'Implemented accessible React forms and connected submissions to REST endpoints with validation feedback.' Keep it true to the actual code and be ready to explain it.","What if accessibility was not tested?","Do not claim it was accessible; describe the semantic labels implemented and note what remains unverified."],
        ["Easy","Prepare a short application note","An application has an optional note field. Draft a concise message that connects one relevant project to the role and does not repeat the entire resume.","Mention the role, one verified skill/project match, and interest in the team's stated work. Keep it direct and professional; omit generic praise and unsupported claims.","Should you address the recruiter by a guessed name?","No; use a neutral greeting or verify the person's name first."],
        ["Easy","Check application completeness","Before submitting, list a quick check for role/location, attachment, contact details, required fields, and external link safety.","Verify the exact position and work location, review the latest resume attachment, confirm contact fields and required responses, check that links point to intended public artifacts, and save a copy of the submitted details where appropriate.","Why recheck the attachment?","A correct file can be selected from the wrong version or contain private metadata."],
        ["Medium","Maintain an application tracker","Design a minimal tracker for job applications that supports follow-up but avoids collecting unnecessary sensitive information.","Store company, role, verified URL/source, date, stage, contact method, next action/date, and a short note. Avoid passwords, identity documents, or private recruiter correspondence; restrict access and delete stale records by policy.","What state should represent an unverified listing?","A separate research/verification state before application submission."],
        ["Medium","Write a professional follow-up","It has been 10 business days since an application and the posting gave no timeline. Draft a respectful follow-up and explain when not to send repeated messages.","Send one brief note through the official channel, restate role and submission date, express continued interest, and ask whether there is an update. Respect any stated timeline and avoid repeated pressure or personal channels.","What if the employer said no status requests?","Follow the instruction and wait for their stated update path."],
        ["Medium","Explain a non-matching qualification","A required tool is unfamiliar but you have built a similar workflow in another framework. Decide how to answer the application question honestly and show transferable evidence.","Do not claim experience with the tool. State the adjacent experience if the field allows context, describe the transferable concept, and be prepared to explain the ramp-up plan. Answer yes/no fields literally.","Can a cover letter replace a required license?","No. Do not imply equivalence for a mandatory credential."],
        ["Hard","Handle duplicate applications safely","A candidate finds the same role on an aggregator and the employer's site and is unsure whether to apply twice. Explain how to verify the canonical route and prevent duplicate submissions.","Use the employer careers site as the source of truth, compare requisition IDs and location, and check tracker/email history before submitting. If already submitted, contact the official recruiting channel only if the portal provides no status and a duplicate would matter.","What if the requisition IDs differ but titles match?","Treat them as potentially separate openings and compare team/location/scope before deciding."],
        ["Hard","Respond to an accommodation request","An online assessment interface is incompatible with a candidate's assistive technology. Explain how to request an accommodation while preserving privacy and application deadlines.","Contact the employer's verified accommodation channel promptly, state the functional barrier and needed adjustment without oversharing diagnosis, document the request, and ask about deadline handling. Keep a copy of communications and follow the official process.","Should the candidate abandon the application immediately?","Not before asking for an accessible alternative or deadline adjustment, unless they choose to withdraw."],
        ["Hard","Protect against application fraud","A recruiter sends an offer before an interview and requests an upfront equipment payment. Plan the response and evidence-preserving steps.","Do not pay or deposit a check; verify the role and recruiter through official company contacts, preserve messages/headers, and report through the platform/company channel. Avoid sharing additional identity or financial details.","Why not trust an email domain that looks similar?","Lookalike domains can be registered; independently navigate to the employer's official site and contact details."],
    ],
    Networking: [
        ["Easy","Ask for an informational conversation","You found an alum working in a role you are exploring. Draft a respectful 15-minute conversation request with context and an easy decline path.","Mention the shared context, one focused reason for reaching out, a time-limited request, and that you understand if they are unavailable. Do not begin by demanding a referral.","What if they do not reply?","One polite follow-up after a reasonable interval is enough; then move on."],
        ["Easy","Offer a useful introduction","A colleague asks to meet someone in your network. Explain how to make a double-opt-in introduction that respects both people's time and privacy.","Ask each person privately first, share a brief purpose and relevant context, and introduce only after both agree. Avoid disclosing contact details or confidential background without consent.","What if one person declines?","Respect the decline and do not reveal it unnecessarily or forward their details."],
        ["Easy","Thank someone after a conversation","After a mentor gives resume feedback, write a concise thank-you that names one useful insight and avoids creating an obligation.","Thank them promptly, mention a specific point you will act on, and close warmly. Do not imply they promised ongoing mentorship or a referral.","Should you report back after using the advice?","A brief update is thoughtful if appropriate, but do not demand another meeting."],
        ["Medium","Prepare focused informational questions","You have 15 minutes with a backend engineer. Select questions that reveal day-to-day work, skill expectations, and team process without asking for confidential information.","Ask about typical work, how junior engineers contribute, which concepts matter most, and how the team reviews/operates services. Avoid confidential product details and spend more time listening than pitching.","How can you make the conversation feel reciprocal?","Share a concise context for your question and thank them; do not force a sales pitch."],
        ["Medium","Reconnect after a long gap","You want to contact a former classmate after two years because they work at a target company. Write an honest message that acknowledges the gap and does not pretend the outreach is unrelated to your job search.","Acknowledge the time gap, mention the genuine shared context, state that you are exploring a role at their company, and ask for a brief conversation only if comfortable. Make it easy to decline and do not assume an internal referral.","Should you attach a resume immediately?","Ask first or share a public link only if requested and permission settings are reviewed."],
        ["Medium","Build a peer learning circle","Three peers want to practice interviews but have different schedules and experience. Propose a simple structure that gives each person fair practice and useful feedback.","Rotate interviewer/candidate/observer roles, use a shared rubric, timebox sessions, choose one goal per meeting, and agree on confidentiality and attendance expectations. Invite feedback on behavior rather than personality.","How do you prevent one experienced person from dominating?","Rotate roles and allocate equal speaking/practice time."],
        ["Hard","Respond to a networking request you cannot satisfy","A new contact asks you to recommend them for a role you do not know enough about. Explain how to respond kindly without making a misleading endorsement.","Be transparent that you cannot provide a meaningful recommendation yet, offer a conversation or public application path if appropriate, and ask for evidence only if you genuinely intend to review it. Do not forward a referral automatically.","Can you refer them after one quick message?","Only if referral policy allows it and you can honestly vouch for the relevant qualifications."],
        ["Hard","Maintain relationships without transactional spam","You want to stay connected with five people you met at a conference. Create a low-effort plan that provides value and respects their attention.","Record consented context, send one personalized follow-up, share relevant resources only when useful, and check in occasionally with a genuine update. Do not automate repeated generic messages or treat contacts as referral inventory.","What if a person stops responding?","Stop outreach after a polite final follow-up and respect their boundary."],
        ["Hard","Navigate a confidential job search","A candidate's current employer follows them online; they want to network discreetly. Recommend steps that protect privacy without deception or misuse of employer resources.","Review profile/activity visibility, use personal devices/accounts and non-work time, avoid posting confidential employer information, and communicate privately through appropriate channels. Do not misrepresent identity or misuse company contact lists.","Should they lie if asked directly by a coworker?","They can set a truthful boundary, such as saying they are exploring options privately, without sharing details."],
    ],
    "Technical Screening": [
        ["Easy","Clarify an ambiguous coding prompt","An interviewer says 'remove duplicates from this list' without specifying order or equality. List the clarification questions you should ask before choosing an algorithm.","Ask whether to preserve first-seen order, whether values are primitives or objects, what equality means, whether input may be mutated, and expected size. State assumptions if the interviewer wants you to proceed.","What default is reasonable for a list of primitive IDs?","Preserve first occurrence and use Set equality, while stating that assumption."],
        ["Easy","Explain complexity in plain language","You scan an array once and use a set of seen values. Explain time and space complexity to a non-specialist and state the assumption behind the time claim.","Work grows roughly with the number of entries, so expected O(n) time, and the set may store up to n values, so O(n) extra space. Hash lookups are expected constant time, not a worst-case guarantee in every implementation.","How does the answer change if memory is constrained?","Sorting can reduce extra memory in some settings but changes order and costs O(n log n)."],
        ["Easy","Test an edge case before submitting","A function returns the first match in a list. Name representative and edge tests and explain what each catches.","Test a match at the beginning, a match at the end, no match, empty input, and duplicate matches if ordering matters. These check boundary loops, failure return, and first-versus-any-match semantics.","Why test a single-element input?","It exercises both boundary behavior and a minimal non-empty case."],
        ["Medium","Debug a failing boundary condition","Your binary search passes normal cases but loops forever when two candidate indices remain. Explain how to reproduce, inspect, and correct it.","Use a two-element case where the target is absent or at the last index, trace midpoint and bounds, and ensure each branch moves beyond mid or contracts a half-open interval. Add the regression test before resubmitting.","What loop invariant helps catch the defect?","The candidate interval contains every possible target index and must strictly shrink each iteration."],
        ["Medium","Respond when a test reveals a bug","During a live screen, an edge test fails after you said the algorithm was complete. Describe how to recover professionally and efficiently.","Acknowledge the failure, state expected/actual results, isolate the smallest cause, update the code, and rerun both failing and normal cases. Communicate uncertainty and protect remaining time for complexity review.","Should you keep coding silently until it is fixed?","No. Concise narration lets the interviewer follow reasoning and offer useful guidance."],
        ["Medium","Choose data structure from operation costs","A task scheduler repeatedly inserts priorities and removes the highest priority; items do not need random indexing. Explain why a heap is a better fit than repeatedly sorting the entire list.","A binary heap supports O(log n) insertion and removal of the extreme plus O(1) peek, while sorting after every insert wastes repeated work. A balanced tree may be preferable if ordered iteration or arbitrary removal is also required.","What if priorities change after insertion?","Use an indexed heap with update support or lazy invalidation/versioned entries, depending on access patterns."],
        ["Hard","Handle a constraint change mid-interview","You propose an O(n log n) solution, then the interviewer says the input is already sorted and memory must be O(1). Explain how you revisit the approach without defending the original unnecessarily.","Restate the new constraints, identify which work becomes redundant, and explore a two-pointer/streaming method with constant auxiliary state if the task permits. Check mutation and output requirements before claiming the new bound.","What if output itself is required as a new array?","Clarify whether output storage is excluded from auxiliary space; producing a copy requires O(n) output memory."],
        ["Hard","Compare a greedy and dynamic programming approach","A coin-change prompt asks for minimum coins. Explain how you would decide whether greedy is valid for the given denominations and when dynamic programming is safer.","Do not assume greedy for arbitrary denominations: [1,3,4] amount 6 gives greedy 4+1+1 but optimum 3+3. For arbitrary positive coins, DP over amounts gives O(amount×coins); prove a canonical-system property before using greedy.","What if the amount is extremely large?","Ask about constraints and consider graph/number-theoretic or bounded-coin methods only if justified; standard amount DP may be infeasible."],
        ["Hard","Explain a solution under time pressure","You have five minutes left and a correct implementation but have not explained complexity or limitations. Structure a concise close-out that helps the interviewer assess the work.","State the invariant and proof idea, time/space bounds, assumptions, and one untested edge. Run a final representative test; do not claim production readiness or hide known limitations to save face.","What if the implementation is still incomplete?","Describe the completed portion, remaining risk, and next step honestly rather than presenting pseudocode as finished code."],
    ],
    "Offer Preparation": [
        ["Easy","Compare written compensation components","An offer lists base salary, a one-time joining bonus, and a discretionary annual bonus. Explain which items are guaranteed and what clarification to request before comparing it with another offer.","Separate recurring base from one-time and discretionary amounts. Ask for written bonus eligibility/criteria, payment timing, and any repayment condition; do not treat a discretionary bonus as guaranteed total compensation.","What document should confirm the final terms?","The formal written offer and applicable plan/policy documents."],
        ["Easy","Check the offer deadline","An offer says 'respond soon' but gives no date. Draft a professional request for a clear decision deadline and explain why you should avoid inventing urgency.","Thank the employer, state your interest, and ask when they need a decision. If you need time for review, request a specific reasonable extension and wait for confirmation.","Should you accept before reading the full terms?","No. Review role, compensation, conditions, location, and contingencies first."],
        ["Easy","Confirm role and work arrangement","The verbal offer says hybrid, but the written document says onsite in another city. Describe the next step before accepting.","Ask the recruiter to clarify and correct the written terms, including work location and expected schedule. Rely on confirmed documentation rather than assuming the verbal summary overrides it.","What else may depend on work location?","Payroll, tax, relocation, and employment eligibility requirements."],
        ["Medium","Compare equity offers carefully","Two offers describe equity: one gives an option count, the other a percentage estimate. List the additional details needed to compare them responsibly.","Ask about instrument type, fully diluted share count or denominator, vesting/cliff, strike price where relevant, exercise window, dilution, liquidity/tax considerations, and plan documents. Equity value is uncertain and not equivalent to cash salary.","Can you assign a guaranteed cash value to private-company options?","No. State assumptions and uncertainty; consult qualified tax/financial advice for personal decisions."],
        ["Medium","Negotiate using priorities and evidence","You want higher compensation but also care about mentorship and location. Describe a respectful negotiation that is specific and leaves room for a package tradeoff.","Express enthusiasm, cite relevant market/experience evidence if available, make a clear request, and identify alternative levers such as start date or location only if they matter. Ask rather than issue an ultimatum and evaluate the full package.","What if the employer says base salary is fixed?","Ask whether another valued component is flexible and decide based on your priorities."],
        ["Medium","Evaluate benefits beyond salary","An offer has lower base pay but strong health coverage, paid leave, and learning support. Create a comparison checklist and identify details that should be verified in writing.","Compare total cash and benefits, eligibility/waiting periods, employee premiums, leave rules, learning budget conditions, hours, commute, and role growth. Use your own needs and verify plan documents rather than relying on a headline summary.","Why can an advertised benefit be misleading?","Eligibility, caps, vesting, or reimbursement rules may change its actual value."],
        ["Hard","Respond to pressure for an immediate signature","A recruiter says the offer expires in two hours, but the written terms are incomplete and you have another interview scheduled. Plan a response that preserves professionalism and protects informed consent.","Ask for the missing terms and a reasonable written deadline. Explain you need time to review; do not sign terms you do not understand. Decide based on confirmed information and be prepared to decline if pressure prevents due diligence.","What if the role truly has a short hiring deadline?","Request the essential terms promptly and make a considered decision; urgency does not remove the need to understand obligations."],
        ["Hard","Review restrictive clauses responsibly","An offer includes confidentiality, IP assignment, and a non-solicitation clause you do not understand. Explain how to handle the uncertainty without giving legal advice or signing blindly.","Ask the employer for clarification in writing, review the full agreement and jurisdiction, and consult a qualified employment lawyer if the obligations are material. Do not assume the summary overrides contract language.","Can an online template determine whether a clause is enforceable?","No. Enforceability depends on jurisdiction and specific facts; obtain qualified advice."],
        ["Hard","Decline an offer while preserving trust","You accept another role and need to decline a second offer. Draft a timely and respectful response without criticizing the team or leaving the employer uncertain.","Thank them, clearly state that you are declining, and if appropriate give a brief truthful reason such as accepting another opportunity. Notify the recruiter promptly, return or delete confidential materials as instructed, and avoid making promises you cannot keep.","What if you already signed the offer?","Review the agreement and seek qualified advice about obligations before taking action; communicate carefully and promptly."],
    ]
};

const slug = (value) => value.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-").replace(/^-|-$/g, "");
const exampleFor = (question, answer, label) => ({ input: question, output: answer, explanation: `${label}: use the reference to check completeness, then answer in your own accurate words.` });

export const FULL_STACK_JOB_PREPARATION_PRACTICE = Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(jobPrepCases).map(([topic, items]) => [topic, items.map(([difficulty,title,question,answer,followUp,edge],index) => ({
    id: `fs-job-prep-${slug(topic)}-${difficulty.toLowerCase()}-${index+1}-v1`, career: "Full Stack Developer", phase: "Job Preparation", topic, title, difficulty,
    estimatedTime: difficulty === "Easy" ? "15–20 minutes" : difficulty === "Medium" ? "25–35 minutes" : "35–50 minutes", kind: "written", language: "Career preparation response",
    simplerProblem: `Draft a concise response for the ${topic} scenario before reviewing the tradeoffs.`,
    problemStatement: `${question} Prepare a practical response, check it against the requirements, and answer the follow-up scenario.`,
    requirements: ["Address the specific scenario and give a concrete next step.","Use truthful evidence and do not invent credentials, outcomes, or employer details.","Explain relevant tradeoffs, privacy or safety constraints, and the stated edge case."],
    examples: [exampleFor(question,answer,"Reference response"),exampleFor(followUp,edge,"Follow-up response")],
    tests: [{name:"Scenario response",input:question,expected:answer},{name:"Edge-case response",input:followUp,expected:edge}],
    hints: [`Identify the actual decision or risk in this ${topic} scenario before drafting.`,`State what is known, what needs verification, and a realistic next action.`],
    learningObjectives:[`Apply ${topic} practices to a realistic career-preparation scenario.`,`Communicate decisions accurately while protecting privacy and professional trust.`],
    conceptsToReview:[topic,"Evidence-based communication","Professional judgment"], prerequisites:[],
    approach:`Separate verified facts from assumptions, answer the immediate ${topic} decision, and identify a safe next step that fits the candidate's real experience and constraints.`,
    walkthrough:`Reference response: ${answer} Follow-up: ${followUp} Consideration: ${edge} Explain why the recommendation fits the stated scenario.`,
    solutionCode:`Reference answer:\n${answer}\n\nFollow-up:\n${followUp}\n\nReview: keep claims accurate, protect personal information, and confirm important terms or facts through an appropriate source.`,
    solutionLines:[answer,followUp,edge],commonMistakes:["Using a generic script that ignores the specific situation.","Presenting an assumption, future plan, or estimate as a verified fact."],
    followUp,completionCriteria:["The response addresses the stated scenario directly.","Claims are truthful and scoped to available evidence.","The follow-up or edge case is handled with a practical action."],
    starterCode:`Scenario:\n\nMy response:\n\nEvidence or assumptions:\n\nNext action:\n\nFollow-up:`
}))]));
